    },
    {
      "id": "CRIT-LUMEN-073",
      "criterion": "WHEN compute_grounding_score is executed, THE SYSTEM SHALL compute the ratio of tool argument values grounded in prior user or tool outputs versus hallucinated values.",
      "is_error_case": false,
      "judgment_call": false
    },
    {
      "id": "CRIT-LUMEN-074",
      "criterion": "IF a transcript has 0 or 1 turns, THE SYSTEM SHALL return monotonicity M = 1.0 and grounding score = 1.0 without dividing by zero.",
      "is_error_case": true,
      "judgment_call": false
    },
    {
      "id": "CRIT-LUMEN-155",
      "criterion": "WHEN calculate_monotonicity(transcript) is invoked on a transcript containing 3 consecutive tool invocations that share the same grounded target file or symbol with no interleaving mutation, THE SYSTEM SHALL return M < 1.0, reflecting a detected circular exploration loop.",
      "is_error_case": false,
      "judgment_call": false
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-LUMEN-006",
  "linked_research": "RES-LUMEN-001",
  "spec_file_path": "specs/SPEC-LUMEN-006-PATTERN.json",
  "reasoning": "Establishes graph-theoretical trajectory modeling, Tarjan SCC cycle detection, and mathematical quality indicators. CRIT-LUMEN-072 was rewritten (same id) to bind it to the real compute_recovery_index function/formula (crates/lumen-pattern/src/lib.rs:141-166) instead of untestable prose; the formula was already correctly implemented, only the api_surface entry and criterion wording were missing. CRIT-LUMEN-155 is a known-failing (red) criterion as of 2026-08-19: the public calculate_monotonicity(transcript) entry point (lib.rs:169-185) currently constructs every ToolNode with target_symbol: None / target_file: None, so detect_circular_loops (lib.rs:65-84) never fires. Fixing this requires TWO changes, not one: (1) populating target_symbol/target_file from the tool call's grounded path/symbol data, AND (2) adding back-edge construction logic to TrajectoryGraph::push_tool (or an equivalent graph-building step) that links a node back to a prior node sharing the same target_symbol/target_file when no interleaving mutation occurred — field population alone is insufficient, since push_tool (lib.rs:50-63) only ever adds a single forward edge per call, producing a strictly linear chain with no cycles regardless of node content (confirmed by crates/lumen-pattern/tests/test_graph_topology.rs's test_3_node_cycles_on_identical_symbols, which only detects a cycle after manually adding a back-edge via g.graph.add_edge(n3, n1, ())). CRIT-075 must not be marked satisfied without both changes. Real-data research pass, 2026-08-20: OpenHands' real stuck-detector (a mature, 84k-star agent framework's production loop-detection code, not an analogy) uses five fixed pattern checks (identical action+observation repeat, error-streak-with-a-one-shot-corrective-nudge, agent monologue with no user interruption, period-2 A-B-A-B alternation specifically, and a context-window-error loop OpenHands itself marks as an unsolved TODO) rather than general graph-cycle detection. This confirms Lumen's Tarjan-SCC approach is directionally sound and, in one real dimension, MORE general (arbitrary-length cycles vs. OpenHands' fixed patterns) -- but OpenHands covers two failure categories Tarjan-SCC has no equivalent of: agent monologue (no user interruption for N consecutive turns) and live mid-loop intervention (a corrective nudge, not just post-hoc detection). No criteria are added for these here -- flagged as a possible follow-up spec addition if the user wants monologue/error-streak detection, not added speculatively without that decision."
}

